	"astrobear/abaplorer/features/tableCompare.feature.js":function(){
sap.ui.define([],()=>{"use strict";return{id:"tableCompare",tabs:{tableCompare:{name:"Compare Tables",viewName:"astrobear.abaplorer.view.CompareTables",icon:"sap-icon://compare-2"}},nav:[{group:"bwBcs",tab:"tableCompare",order:20,text:"Compare Tables",tooltip:"Regression test — compare the contents of two tables or two sheets",icon:"sap-icon://compare-2"}],tiles:[{header:"Compare Tables",subheader:"Compare two sets of data — database tables, Excel sheets, or one against the other",icon:"sap-icon://compare-2",viewName:"astrobear.abaplorer.view.CompareTables",order:240,functionality:`Two tools under one tab. **Database Tables** is the regression test over two structurally identical tables — table A against table B, e.g. before and after a BW/4HANA migration — and lists the rows that exist on only one of the two sides.\n        It reads the DDIC structure of both tables, lets you exclude single fields (technical load fields like REQTSN or RECORD are excluded by default), and reports row counts per table plus the rows found only in table A and only in table B. Every differing row names the side it came from and the table name entered for it. The two tables may also live in different systems — with a Rainbow connection table B is read on the remote system. The generated statement can be handed over to the SQL Console.\n        **Excel Sheets** compares two uploaded .xlsx or .csv files with no system involved at all — or a sheet against a database table, here or on a Rainbow-connected system: map the columns of the one onto the other, then aggregate and group them, list the records missing on either side, or crosscheck them key by key down to the single cell. **Filter B by A** is the lookup among them: tick the key columns, and the rows of B whose key occurs in A are listed with all of B's columns — with B a table, A's keys go straight into the WHERE clause, so only the matching rows are read. Both results export as CSV/XLSX.`,usecase:"Verify that ZBW4_EC100_SM and ZBW4_EC100_B4 hold the same data after the migration — reconcile a legacy export against a spreadsheet the customer sent, without loading either into a system first — or pull exactly the table rows that belong to the period/item/currency combinations listed in a sheet."}]}});
},
